  Not settled: [1mnothing in this app produces a [38;5;153muserId[39m today.[0m The form collects [38;5;153musername[39m and [38;5;153mpassword[39m only ([38;5;153mindex.html[39m),
  there's no session, no storage, no auth response to read an id from. So "add a userId parameter" needs a source for
  the value, and the choice of source changes what you're actually tracking.

  The fork: whether [38;5;153muserId[39m identifies [3mthe person[0m (a stable identity you'd have to introduce from somewhere) or [3mthis [0m
  [3mlogin attempt[0m (an id you mint client-side at call time).

  - [1mMint a client-side id per attempt[0m — one line in the handler ([38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m), no new UI, no new state. Buys you
    correlation of a login attempt across logs. Costs: it is not a person, so it can't answer "who logged in" across
    sessions. Cheap to replace later.
  - [1mAdd a [38;5;153muserId[39m field to the login form[0m — the user types it. Buys a real person-identifier with no backend work. Costs:
    a UI change in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m, and a self-asserted id is trustworthy only if the backend validates it. Changes the
    form, so slightly less cheap to undo.
  - [1mDerive it from the login response instead[0m — the server returns the id after authenticating. This is the correct
    answer for real identity, but it means [38;5;153muserId[39m is a [3mreturn value[0m, not a parameter, so it contradicts the request as
    written. Costs: depends on a backend that doesn't exist in this stub.

  My recommendation is the first one, with the parameter named for what it is. Given [38;5;153mlogin[39m is a stub that only
  [38;5;153mconsole.log[39ms, a per-attempt id is the only option that's honest about what this code can currently know — the other
  two promise an identity the app has no way to establish yet. If you want real user identity, that's a larger piece of
  work than a parameter and I'd want to scope it separately.
